ously the liquid  biopsy has a lot of advantages and in  many case taking a biopsy is dead  there's just it's unavailable or it  almost unethical to to do to these  patients and late-stage metastatic  disease um the test is is not  sequencing-based so you know I think  this is a key differentiator when you  think about how we take this test and  and have a quicker turnaround time as  well as globally deploy this and its  application as a diagnostic this test  has key advantages over sequencing in  the fact that that a lot of labs around  the world can do and  I have stained and and so simple blood  draw we can enable longitudinal  monitoring and so those are the key  differentiating factors so I see this  fitting into the landscape that we're  able to differentiate by addressing some  of the complications associated with  finding hrd and metastatic disease thank  you how does the prevalence of cr.pc hrd  positive patient in your study compared  to those of the top heart study yeah  that's a great question the top our  study was actually in third line  patients mcrpc patients and those  patients were pre-screened for not only  disease progression but the prevalence  of over or greater than five CTCs per  mil and as we showed earlier the  patients who tend to resist the air  targeted therapies and chemo therapies  that they tend to do hrd positive  patients tend to do worse on those  therapies and so there it's possible  that it's your enriching for these  patients in such a late stage setting  whereas I think the data we were shown  was much earlier in a first line setting  where these patients hadn't developed  resistance mechanisms to a our targeted  therapies or taxanes and and I think  that they had noticed or observed and  reported thirty-three percent prevalence  of hrd in this late stage and third line  we saw the prevalence of the markers  about forty percent so those very  similar numbers and then is the key to  this is that there were several patients  that were excluded from taupe Harbor  that were excluded because you couldn't  get tumor biopsy or you didn't see CTCs  and so when you think about that we're  not excluding patients if they didn't  have CTCs they were biomarker negative  and so I think this is some of the  highlights really some of the advantages  of a liquid biopsy but also highlights  some of these  vantages of using the phenotypic  analysis and compared us into genomic  sequencing thank you look like we have  time for one last question what is the  impact of heterogeneity on the stage  accuracy yeah and I think this is really  critical not only to this marker but  also just across all metastatic disease  and late stage disease and so as we  showed that there's a high degree of  heterogeneity in these patients and do  much worse on the targeted therapies but  when you think about it in the context  of hrd we saw sub clonal populations of  hrd present in a lot of the patients who  showed some response and so these  patients when you think about the under  sampling that can happen with the tissue  biopsy and other complications  associated with there being multiple sub  clonal drivers and that effect on an  interpretation of the sequencing results  you what you can see is that it's likely  to have an impact in selecting these  patients and so I think the single-cell  resolution of this and the ability to  look at the cells both phenotypically  and genotypically at the single-cell  level is really what's driving the  selection of these patients who may be a  sub clonal disease and could show some  response to the parp inhibitor versus  missing these patients due to sampling  air or having the heterogeneity to too  high that they can't see the signal in  the tumor biopsy and so I yeah I think  that's any other questions or well now I  just wanted to know use your final  comments for today's presentations yeah  and so you know we will address any  additional questions so please feel free  to shoot additio